# Long-form devotionals (original). Blocks: h = heading, p = paragraph (supports **bold** / *italic*), v = scripture reference (resolved from WEB).
DEVOTIONALS = {
# ======================= DAY 1 =======================
"1-1-1": [
 {"h": "Enough Light for the Next Step"},
 {"p": "Picture a hiker on a mountain trail an hour before sunrise. The path is steep, the air is cold, and the only light she has comes from a small lamp strapped to her forehead. It doesn’t light up the whole mountain. It doesn’t show her the summit, the switchbacks ahead, or the view waiting at the top. It shows about three steps — **and three steps are enough to keep walking.**"},
 {"p": "Most of us wish God worked differently. We want the whole map: where our career is heading, how a relationship will turn out, whether the diagnosis will change, what the next five years will look like. We tell ourselves that if we could just *see* it all, we would finally feel at peace. But that is rarely how God leads His people. Again and again in Scripture, **God gives light for the next step, not for the entire journey.**"},
 {"p": "Abraham was told to go to “the land I will show you” — without being told where it was. Israel followed a cloud by day and fire by night, one campsite at a time. The disciples were simply told, “Follow me.” None of them received a detailed plan. What they received was a Person to follow and enough light to obey today."},
 {"p": "*Psalm 119:105* puts it this way:"},
 {"v": "Psalm 119:105"},
 {"p": "In the ancient world, a lamp was a small clay bowl filled with olive oil, with a wick resting on its edge. Travelers at night would hold it low, close to their feet, so they wouldn’t trip on rocks or fall into a ditch. The psalmist chose that image on purpose. God’s Word is not a floodlight that removes every mystery from life. It is **a faithful lamp that keeps you from stumbling** — close, steady, and always enough for the step in front of you."},
 {"h": "The Light Has a Name"},
 {"p": "Centuries later, Jesus stood in the temple courts during the Feast of Tabernacles. Every night of that festival, enormous golden lampstands were lit, and it was said that their glow could be seen across Jerusalem. Into that setting, Jesus made one of the boldest statements anyone has ever made:"},
 {"v": "John 8:12"},
 {"p": "Notice what He did not say. He did not say, “I will give you some light,” or “I will show you the way to the light.” He said, **“I am the light.”** Light is not just something Jesus offers; it is who He is. That changes the question we ask. Instead of “God, show me the whole plan,” we begin to ask, “Lord, how do I stay close to You today?”"},
 {"p": "And look carefully at the promise: whoever follows Him “will not walk in the darkness.” It doesn’t say darkness will disappear from the world. Hard seasons still come. Questions still keep us awake at night. But we will not be *walking in* the darkness, because the Light walks with us."},
 {"h": "Reflecting on the Light: Two Truths to Hold"},
 {"p": "When you feel lost or unsure about what comes next, here are two truths worth holding on to:"},
 {"p": "1. **God’s guidance is usually step by step.** If you are waiting for the full picture before you obey, you may wait forever. Faith often looks like doing the next right thing — reading, praying, forgiving, showing up — and trusting that the next step will be lit when you need it. God is not withholding the map to frustrate you; He is inviting you to walk *with* Him instead of simply walking *ahead* by yourself."},
 {"p": "2. **Darkness never has the final word.** Whatever you are facing — anxiety at night, grief that lingers, confusion about a decision — the darkness is not stronger than the One who calls Himself the Light. John wrote this at the very beginning of his Gospel:"},
 {"v": "John 1:5"},
 {"p": "The darkness has tried. It has not won. It never will."},
 {"h": "“Ner”: The Lamp at Your Feet"},
 {"p": "The Hebrew word for “lamp” in Psalm 119 is **ner** (נֵר). It is the same word used for the lamp that burned in the tabernacle, the one the priests were told to keep lit continually before the Lord. A *ner* was not dramatic. It didn’t blaze; it glowed. But it was faithful. It burned through the whole night if someone kept adding oil."},
 {"p": "That is a beautiful picture of how God’s Word works in an ordinary life. Most days, reading Scripture won’t feel like fireworks. It will feel like a small, steady glow. But as you keep returning to it — a few verses in the morning, a prayer at night — **the lamp keeps burning**, and you find that you have enough light to take the next step, and the next, and the next."},
 {"h": "Your Step Today"},
 {"p": "Maybe you opened this app because something in your life feels dark or uncertain. Maybe you simply want to know God more. Either way, here is the good news: you don’t need to fix everything today. You don’t need to understand every chapter of the Bible or have a perfect prayer life. You just need to take one step toward the Light."},
 {"p": "What does that look like? It might mean reading today’s verse again before bed. It might mean telling God honestly, “I don’t know what’s next, but I want to follow You.” It might mean making one decision today based on what you already know is right, even if you can’t see where it leads."},
 {"p": "**You don’t need the whole map. You need the Light. And the Light is already with you.**"},
 {"p": "Great job finishing today’s lesson! Tomorrow we’ll discover something even more personal: the God who lights your path also knows you — and calls you — by name."}
],
# ======================= DAY 2 =======================
"1-1-2": [
 {"h": "Known in a Crowd"},
 {"p": "Think about the last time someone remembered your name when you didn’t expect it. Maybe it was a teacher you hadn’t seen in years, a barista who greeted you before you ordered, or a colleague from another department who said, “Hi, Sarah!” as you passed. Something small happens inside you in that moment. **I was noticed. I matter here.**"},
 {"p": "Now think about the opposite feeling — standing in a crowded room where no one knows you, or sending a message that never gets an answer, or wondering if anyone would notice if you simply stopped showing up. Many people carry that feeling quietly for years. They are surrounded by people, yet they feel unseen."},
 {"p": "Into that feeling, Scripture speaks a message that is almost too good to believe: **the God who made the stars knows your name** — not as data, not as a number, but personally, warmly, the way a parent knows the name of a child."},
 {"p": "God spoke these words through the prophet Isaiah to a people who felt forgotten. They were far from home, surrounded by a culture that didn’t share their faith, wondering if God had moved on without them. His answer was not a lecture. It was a name. *Isaiah 43:1* tells us:"},
 {"v": "Isaiah 43:1"},
 {"p": "Look at the order of that verse. First, God reminds them who He is: the One who **created** and **formed** them. Then He tells them what He has done: He has **redeemed** them. And finally, He says what they are: **“You are mine.”** Their identity didn’t come from their circumstances, their failures, or what others said about them. It came from the One who called them by name."},
 {"h": "The Shepherd Who Calls"},
 {"p": "Jesus took this truth and wrapped it in a picture everyone in His day would understand. In ancient Israel, several families often kept their flocks together in one stone sheepfold overnight. In the morning, each shepherd would come to the gate and call out. The sheep would lift their heads — and only his sheep would come, because they recognized his voice."},
 {"v": "John 10:3"},
 {"p": "Some shepherds even had a name for each sheep, often based on a marking or a personality trait. That is the picture Jesus chose to describe His relationship with you. **You are not lost in the flock.** He knows the details — your history, your fears, the things you’ve never said out loud — and He still calls you by name."},
 {"h": "Reflecting on Being Known: Two Truths to Remember"},
 {"p": "1. **You are known completely — and loved completely.** Many of us fear that if someone truly knew us, they would walk away. With God, the opposite is true. He already knows everything, and He stays. David marveled at this in Psalm 139: God knows when he sits and when he rises, his thoughts from far away, every word before it’s spoken. Being fully known by God is not a threat; it is the safest place in the world."},
 {"v": "Psalm 139:1"},
 {"p": "2. **Your identity is given, not earned.** In a world that constantly asks “What do you do?” and “What have you achieved?”, God speaks a different word over you: *“You are mine.”* Your worth is not the sum of your performance, your appearance, or your popularity. It rests on the fact that you belong to Him."},
 {"h": "“Qara”: To Call by Name"},
 {"p": "The Hebrew word translated “called” in Isaiah 43:1 is **qara** (קָרָא). It means to call out, to summon, to proclaim — but when it’s used with someone’s name, it carries the idea of claiming and appointing them. In the ancient world, naming something was an act of relationship and responsibility. When God says, “I have called you by your name,” He is saying, **“I have claimed you. You are my responsibility. I will take care of you.”**"},
 {"p": "That is why the very next verse continues with a promise: when you pass through the waters, He will be with you; when you walk through the fire, you will not be burned. The God who calls you by name doesn’t call you and then leave you. He walks with you."},
 {"h": "Learning to Recognize His Voice"},
 {"p": "Recognizing a voice takes time. You learned to recognize your mother’s voice, your best friend’s laugh, your spouse’s footsteps in the hallway — not through one encounter, but through many. The same is true with God. Every time you open His Word, pray honestly, or pause to reflect, **you become more familiar with how He speaks.**"},
 {"v": "John 10:27"},
 {"p": "Notice the three movements in that verse: His sheep **hear**, He **knows**, they **follow**. It’s a relationship, not a formula. You don’t have to be a spiritual expert to hear Him. You just have to keep showing up, keep listening, and keep taking the next step."},
 {"p": "So today, let this settle deep into your heart: **you are not invisible. You are not forgotten. You are known, called, and claimed by God.**"},
 {"p": "Great job finishing today’s lesson! Tomorrow we’ll look at one of the most freeing truths in the Bible — that God’s love came to you before you ever earned it."}
],
# ======================= DAY 3 =======================
"1-1-3": [
 {"h": "The Bill You Keep Trying to Pay"},
 {"p": "Imagine a friend invites you to dinner. When the check arrives, she slides it toward herself and says, “This one’s on me.” A week later, you try to pay her back. She laughs and says, “It was a gift.” A month later, you try again. And again. Eventually, something becomes a little sad: you simply can’t believe someone would be generous with you **without expecting something in return.**"},
 {"p": "Many sincere believers live exactly that way with God. In our heads, we know salvation is a gift. But in our hearts, we keep trying to pay the bill. One more good deed. One more perfect week. One more promise to do better. And when we fail — because we always do — we feel far from God, as if His love had been withdrawn because we missed a payment."},
 {"p": "The Bible tells a very different story. *Romans 5:8* says:"},
 {"v": "Romans 5:8"},
 {"p": "Read that slowly. God did not wait for us to clean ourselves up. He didn’t wait for us to become religious enough, disciplined enough, or good enough. **He showed His love while we were still sinners** — while we were still running in the wrong direction. His love is not a reward for our progress. It is the starting point of it."},
 {"h": "Grace, Then Good Works"},
 {"p": "The apostle Paul spent much of his life as a man who tried to earn God’s approval. He was a Pharisee, meticulous about the law, proud of his record. When he met Jesus, everything turned upside down. Years later, he explained the order of things with remarkable clarity:"},
 {"v": "Ephesians 2:8-9"},
 {"p": "We are saved **by grace, through faith**. Not by effort. Not by a spiritual résumé. Not by being better than the people around us. And Paul draws the obvious conclusion: no one gets to boast. But there is a second conclusion hiding in that verse, one that is just as important — **no one has to despair.** If salvation depended on your performance, you could never be sure you had done enough. Because it depends on God’s grace, you can rest."},
 {"p": "But Paul doesn’t stop there. In the very next sentence, he explains what grace produces:"},
 {"v": "Ephesians 2:10"},
 {"p": "Good works are not the price of God’s love; they are the fruit of it. **A tree doesn’t grow fruit in order to become a tree. It grows fruit because it is one.** When you know you are loved, obedience stops being a heavy obligation and becomes a grateful response."},
 {"h": "Reflecting on Grace: Two Truths That Set You Free"},
 {"p": "1. **God’s love is not a wage.** A wage is something you earn; a gift is something you receive. If you treat God’s love like a paycheck, you will always feel underpaid or overdue. But if you receive it as a gift, you can finally stop measuring yourself every day. Your worst day doesn’t cancel His love, and your best day doesn’t increase it. It was never about the scorecard."},
 {"p": "2. **Grace is not permission to stay the same.** Some people fear that if they stop striving, they’ll stop growing. But grace is not a couch; it’s soil. It is the environment where real change finally becomes possible. People who know they are loved don’t stop doing good — they start doing it for the right reasons."},
 {"h": "“Charis” and “Poiēma”: Gift and Masterpiece"},
 {"p": "The Greek word for grace in Ephesians 2 is **charis** (χάρις). In everyday Greek, it described a favor freely given, a kindness that the giver chose to offer without obligation. It is closely related to *chara*, the word for joy. Grace and joy come from the same family — which makes sense. **Receiving a gift you could never earn naturally leads to joy.**"},
 {"p": "Then, in verse 10, Paul calls us God’s “workmanship.” The Greek word is **poiēma** (ποίημα) — the root of our English word *poem*. It describes something crafted with care and intention, a work of art. You are not God’s project to fix. **You are His poem, still being written.** The good works He prepared for you are not tests to pass; they are lines He wants to write through your life."},
 {"h": "Resting in What Is Finished"},
 {"p": "Why do we keep trying so hard, as if we needed to win God’s affection? Perhaps because we live in a world where almost everything is earned — grades, salaries, approval, even love from some people. Unconditional love feels strange to us. We keep waiting for the catch."},
 {"p": "But the cross is God’s way of saying there is no catch. Jesus paid what you could never pay. When He said, “It is finished,” He meant it. **You can stop trying to settle a bill that has already been paid in full.**"},
 {"p": "Today, take a deep breath and receive what God is offering. Thank Him for a gift you could never afford. Then let that gratitude spill over into kindness toward someone else — not to earn anything, but because you have been loved so well."},
 {"p": "**Grace first. Then everything else.**"},
 {"p": "Great job finishing today’s lesson! Tomorrow we’ll explore what it looks like to come to God honestly in prayer — without pretending, without performing."}
],
# ======================= DAY 4 =======================
"1-1-4": [
 {"h": "Prayer Is Not a Performance"},
 {"p": "A little girl climbs onto her father’s lap at the end of the day and starts talking. She tells him about a scraped knee, a friend who was mean at recess, a drawing she’s proud of, and a strange dream she had the night before. She doesn’t organize her thoughts into neat points. She doesn’t use impressive words. She just pours it all out — **and her father loves every second of it.**"},
 {"p": "Somewhere along the way, many of us learned that prayer has to sound a certain way. We heard others pray with beautiful phrases and long sentences, and our own prayers felt too simple, too messy, or too small. So we either started performing — saying what we thought God wanted to hear — or we stopped praying altogether, except in emergencies."},
 {"p": "But Scripture invites something far simpler and far more real:"},
 {"v": "Psalm 62:8"},
 {"p": "**Pour out your heart.** Not a summary. Not a cleaned-up version. The Psalms are full of raw, honest prayers — joy and grief, fear and anger, confusion and gratitude, sometimes all in the same chapter. God included them in the Bible to show us that **He is not shocked by your feelings. He is a refuge for them.**"},
 {"h": "“Lord, Teach Us to Pray”"},
 {"p": "If prayer came naturally to everyone, the disciples would never have asked for help. But they did. After watching Jesus pray, one of them said, *“Lord, teach us to pray”* (Luke 11:1). Notice: they didn’t ask Him to teach them to preach or to perform miracles. They asked to learn how to talk with the Father. And Jesus answered:"},
 {"v": "Luke 11:2-4"},
 {"p": "In just a few lines, Jesus shows us the whole shape of a healthy prayer life. It begins with **“Father”** — intimacy, not distance. *“May your name be kept holy”* — reverence for who He is. *“May your Kingdom come”* — surrendering our plans to His. *“Give us day by day our daily bread”* — honest dependence for today’s needs. *“Forgive us our sins”* — mercy received and passed on. *“Bring us not into temptation”* — asking for His protection."},
 {"p": "It’s short. It’s simple. And it covers everything that matters. **Jesus never measured prayer by its length, only by its heart.**"},
 {"h": "Thank, Tell, Ask, Trust"},
 {"p": "Drawing from the prayer Jesus taught and the prayers of the Psalms, here is an easy way to remember a balanced conversation with God — four simple words:"},
 {"p": "**T — Thank**"},
 {"p": "Start with gratitude. Before you bring a single request, name something God has already done — a meal, a friend, a breath, a second chance. Gratitude reminds your heart who you’re talking to. Paul writes:"},
 {"v": "1 Thessalonians 5:16-18"},
 {"p": "**T — Tell**"},
 {"p": "Tell God what is really going on inside you — honestly, without polishing it. David didn’t hide his troubles; he named them:"},
 {"v": "Psalm 142:2"},
 {"p": "Confession belongs here too. When you tell God where you’ve fallen short, you’re not informing Him of anything new — you’re opening the door for His mercy to come in."},
 {"p": "**A — Ask**"},
 {"p": "Ask for what you need, big or small. Jesus didn’t say “only ask for spiritual things.” He said:"},
 {"v": "Matthew 7:7"},
 {"p": "Your job interview, your child’s fever, your tight budget, your loneliness on a Friday night — **nothing is too small or too ordinary to bring to God.**"},
 {"p": "**T — Trust**"},
 {"p": "Finally, place the outcome in His hands. This is where prayer becomes rest instead of another way to worry:"},
 {"v": "Philippians 4:6-7"},
 {"p": "The situation may not change immediately. The test results may still be pending. But something inside you changes. **Prayer moves the weight from your shoulders into God’s hands.**"},
 {"h": "“Merimnaō” and “Phroureō”: Divided Minds and Guarded Hearts"},
 {"p": "The Greek word Paul uses for “anxious” is **merimnaō** (μεριμνάω). Many teachers connect it to the idea of being pulled in different directions — a mind divided, torn between a hundred futures that haven’t happened yet. That is exactly what worry feels like."},
 {"p": "Then Paul says God’s peace will **guard** your heart. The word is **phroureō** (φρουρέω), a military term for soldiers standing watch over a city. Paul was writing to Philippi, a Roman colony where soldiers were a familiar sight. The picture is powerful: **when you pray, God stations His peace like soldiers at the gates of your heart.**"},
 {"h": "No Perfect Words Required"},
 {"p": "The beauty of prayer is that, even though it has a shape — thank, tell, ask, trust — the heart of it is not a formula. There are no perfect words you have to say, no special time you have to wait for, no performance to pass. **Prayer is not a ceremony — it’s a conversation with your Father.**"},
 {"p": "Jesus once told a story about two men praying in the temple. One prayed a long, polished prayer about how good he was. The other could barely lift his eyes and simply said, “God, be merciful to me, a sinner.” Jesus said it was the second man who went home right with God. Whether your words are eloquent or broken, whispered or cried out, God hears. And He has promised to answer:"},
 {"v": "Jeremiah 33:3"},
 {"p": "So remember the little girl on her father’s lap. She didn’t need the right words — she just needed to come. **You don’t need perfect words either. You need an honest heart and a Father who listens — and you already have Him.**"},
 {"p": "Great job finishing today’s lesson! Tomorrow we’ll talk about one of the hardest parts of faith: what to do when you’re waiting and God seems quiet."}
],
# ======================= DAY 5 =======================
"1-1-5": [
 {"h": "What Happens Underground"},
 {"p": "There’s an old gardener’s story about a kind of bamboo that, after planting, shows almost nothing above the soil for years. The gardener waters it faithfully, season after season, and sees little more than a small shoot. It would be easy to give up and assume nothing was happening. But underground, something remarkable is taking place: **a massive, interwoven root system is forming.** Then, when the time is right, the shoots rise astonishingly fast — growing many feet in a matter of weeks."},
 {"p": "Waiting seasons can feel like wasted time. You pray and nothing seems to move. You apply and the door stays closed. You hope for healing, for reconciliation, for a breakthrough, and the calendar keeps turning. In those seasons, it’s tempting to conclude that God has forgotten you, or that you must be doing something wrong."},
 {"p": "But Scripture tells us that **God often does His deepest work underground** — in our character, our trust, and our roots — long before anything shows on the surface. *Psalm 27:14* speaks directly to the waiting heart:"},
 {"v": "Psalm 27:14"},
 {"p": "Notice that David doesn’t say, “Wait, and try not to feel anything.” He says, “Be strong, and let your heart take courage.” Waiting on God is not passive. **It takes strength to keep trusting when you can’t see results.** It takes courage to hope again after disappointment."},
 {"h": "Strength for the Long Road"},
 {"p": "Isaiah wrote to people who had waited a very long time — generations, in fact — for God’s promises to be fulfilled. They were tired. Some had stopped believing anything would ever change. Into that weariness, God spoke one of the most beloved promises in the Bible:"},
 {"v": "Isaiah 40:31"},
 {"p": "Look closely at the order: **soar, run, walk.** We tend to read it as if the greatest miracle is to mount up with wings like eagles. But in a long season of waiting, sometimes the greatest miracle is the last one — that you keep walking and don’t faint. On some days God gives you wings. On others, He gives you just enough strength to put one foot in front of the other. **Both are His gift.**"},
 {"h": "Reflecting on Waiting: Two Truths for the In-Between"},
 {"p": "1. **Waiting is not wasted.** Joseph waited years in a prison before he stood in Pharaoh’s palace. Moses spent forty years in the wilderness before he led Israel out of Egypt. David was anointed king as a teenager but spent years running from Saul before he took the throne. In each case, the waiting was not an interruption of God’s plan — **it was part of it.** God was forming the person who could carry the promise."},
 {"p": "2. **God is good to those who wait for Him.** That doesn’t mean everything turns out the way we imagined. It means that God Himself is good, and that He is especially near to those who keep seeking Him in the in-between. Jeremiah wrote these words while sitting in the ruins of Jerusalem:"},
 {"v": "Lamentations 3:25"},
 {"h": "“Qavah”: Waiting With Expectation"},
 {"p": "The Hebrew word translated “wait” in both Psalm 27 and Isaiah 40 is **qavah** (קָוָה). It doesn’t describe passively sitting around. It carries the sense of **eagerly looking for, expecting, hoping** — like a watchman on the city wall scanning the horizon for the first light of dawn. It belongs to the same family of words as *tikvah*, the Hebrew word for hope."},
 {"p": "Many teachers also point out that the root of *qavah* is connected to the idea of a cord or line — strands twisted together until they become strong. It’s a beautiful picture: **as you wait on God, your life is being bound more tightly to His.** What feels like weakness is actually becoming strength."},
 {"h": "Faithful in Today"},
 {"p": "So what do you do while you wait? You stay faithful in the assignments God has already given you. Love the people in front of you. Do your work with excellence. Keep showing up in prayer, even when your prayers feel repetitive. Take care of your body and your soul. **Waiting on God doesn’t mean putting your life on hold — it means living today with your hope anchored in Him.**"},
 {"p": "And remember the gardener’s bamboo. Just because you can’t see growth doesn’t mean nothing is happening. The roots you grow in this season will hold you steady when the season changes."},
 {"p": "**Your waiting is not empty. God is working underground.**"},
 {"p": "Great job finishing today’s lesson! Tomorrow we’ll face one of the most common struggles of the human heart — fear — and discover the command God repeats more than almost any other."}
],
# ======================= DAY 6 =======================
"1-1-6": [
 {"h": "The Most Repeated Command"},
 {"p": "If you read through the Bible looking for God’s most frequent instruction, you might expect something like “try harder” or “be better.” But one of the phrases that appears again and again is much gentler: **“Don’t be afraid.”** God says it to Abraham in the night, to Joshua at the edge of the Promised Land, to Gideon hiding in a winepress, to Mary in Nazareth, to the shepherds in the fields, and to the disciples in a storm."},
 {"p": "Why does God repeat it so often? Because He knows how easily fear takes the steering wheel of our lives. Fear tells us to stay small, to avoid risks, to protect ourselves at all costs. Fear can make us cling to control, avoid difficult conversations, and say no to the very things God is inviting us into."},
 {"p": "Through the prophet Isaiah, God spoke to a people surrounded by threats on every side. *Isaiah 41:10* says:"},
 {"v": "Isaiah 41:10"},
 {"p": "Look carefully at the reasons God gives. He doesn’t say, “Don’t be afraid, because nothing bad will happen.” He says, **“Don’t be afraid, for I am with you. I am your God.”** The cure for fear in the Bible is not the absence of danger. **It is the presence of God.**"},
 {"p": "And then God makes three promises, one after another: *I will strengthen you. I will help you. I will uphold you.* Strength for what is ahead, help in what is now, and a hand that holds you when you feel like falling."},
 {"h": "Courage for the Next Assignment"},
 {"p": "When Moses died, Joshua inherited one of the most overwhelming jobs in history: lead an entire nation across a flooded river into a land full of fortified cities. Joshua had seen giants in that land with his own eyes. If anyone had a reason to be afraid, it was him."},
 {"p": "God didn’t give Joshua a strategy manual first. He gave him a promise of presence:"},
 {"v": "Joshua 1:9"},
 {"p": "Notice that God commands courage — “Be strong and courageous” — but He doesn’t command Joshua to *feel* fearless. **Biblical courage is not the absence of fear; it is moving forward in obedience while the fear is still there**, because you trust the One who walks beside you."},
 {"h": "Reflecting on Fear: Two Things to Remember"},
 {"p": "1. **Fear is a feeling, not a fact.** Fear often predicts disasters that never happen. It speaks in “what ifs” and worst-case scenarios. Feelings are real, and God cares about them — but they are not always reliable guides. When fear speaks, you can answer it with what is true: God is with you, God is for you, and God is able."},
 {"p": "2. **Courage grows by taking small steps.** You don’t have to conquer every fear today. Joshua crossed the Jordan one step at a time, and the priests had to put their feet in the water before it parted. Sometimes God waits for our first step of trust before He shows us His power."},
 {"h": "“Deilia”: The Spirit We Were Not Given"},
 {"p": "Near the end of his life, Paul wrote to his young friend Timothy, who seems to have struggled with timidity. Paul reminded him of something essential:"},
 {"v": "2 Timothy 1:7"},
 {"p": "The Greek word translated “fear” here is **deilia** (δειλία). It refers to timidity or cowardice — the kind of fear that makes us shrink back from what God has called us to do. Paul’s point is clear: **that kind of fear doesn’t come from God.** Instead, the Holy Spirit gives us three things: **power** to face what’s in front of us, **love** that pushes us outward toward others, and **self-control** — a sound, steady mind that isn’t ruled by panic."},
 {"h": "Naming Your Fear"},
 {"p": "Fear grows in the dark and shrinks in the light. One of the most practical things you can do today is to name the fear that has been loudest in your mind. Is it fear of failure? Of being alone? Of not having enough? Of losing someone you love? Of what people think?"},
 {"p": "Once you name it, hold it next to God’s promise. **Fear says, “You’re on your own.” God says, “I am with you.”** Fear says, “You won’t make it.” God says, “I will strengthen you.” Fear says, “It’s all going to fall apart.” God says, “I will uphold you with my righteous right hand.”"},
 {"p": "You get to choose which voice you will follow today."},
 {"p": "**You may still feel afraid. But you are not alone — and that changes everything.**"},
 {"p": "Great job finishing today’s lesson! Tomorrow is our chapter review. We’ll look back at everything God has planted in your heart this week and see how deep your roots have grown."}
],
# ======================= DAY 7 =======================
"1-1-7": [
 {"h": "A Tree by the Water"},
 {"p": "You’ve made it through your first chapter. That is worth celebrating. Building a habit of meeting with God every day is not easy — life is busy, distractions are everywhere, and some days you probably didn’t feel like opening this app. But you kept showing up. **That faithfulness matters more than you know.**"},
 {"p": "Before we move forward, let’s pause and look at what has been planted. The prophet Jeremiah gave us one of the most beautiful pictures of a faith that lasts. *Jeremiah 17:7-8* says:"},
 {"v": "Jeremiah 17:7-8"},
 {"p": "This tree doesn’t live in a world without heat. Drought still comes. The seasons still change. But its leaves stay green and it keeps bearing fruit because **its roots reach the water.** That is the picture of the faith we’re building together — not a faith that never faces hard seasons, but one that stays alive in them."},
 {"p": "Jeremiah contrasts this tree with a shrub in the desert — a person who trusts only in human strength. That shrub has shallow roots and withers when the heat comes. The difference between the two is not the weather. **It’s where the roots go.**"},
 {"h": "What Has Been Planted This Week"},
 {"p": "Let’s look back at the six roots God has been growing in you:"},
 {"p": "1. **Light for the next step.** You don’t need the whole map. God’s Word is a lamp for your feet, and Jesus Himself is the Light of the world."},
 {"p": "2. **Called by name.** You are not invisible or forgotten. God knows you completely, calls you personally, and says, “You are mine.”"},
 {"p": "3. **Grace first.** God’s love is the root, not the reward. He loved you while you were still a sinner, and good works grow as fruit from that love."},
 {"p": "4. **Honest prayer.** You can pour out your heart to God without pretending. Thank, tell, ask, trust — and let His peace stand guard over your mind."},
 {"p": "5. **Active waiting.** Waiting is not wasted. God works underground, and those who hope in Him find renewed strength."},
 {"p": "6. **Courage with presence.** Fear may still come, but you are not alone. God strengthens you, helps you, and holds you up."},
 {"p": "Each of these is a root. Alone, one root might not hold much. **Together, they anchor a life.**"},
 {"h": "“Rhizoō”: Rooted and Built Up"},
 {"p": "Paul wrote to the believers in Colossae, a church surrounded by competing ideas and philosophies. He wanted them to stay steady, so he gave them this encouragement:"},
 {"v": "Colossians 2:6-7"},
 {"p": "The Greek word for “rooted” is **rhizoō** (ῥιζόω) — from *rhiza*, “root.” Paul uses a form of the word that describes something that has already happened and continues to have effects: you **have been rooted**, and you remain rooted. Your roots in Christ are not something you have to create from scratch every morning. God planted them. Your part is to keep drawing from the source."},
 {"p": "Notice, too, how Paul mixes his images. He goes from a tree (*rooted*) to a building (*built up*) to a foundation (*established*). Faith is both organic and structural — **it grows like a tree and stands like a house.** And the sign that it’s healthy? “Abounding in thanksgiving.” A rooted heart is a grateful heart."},
 {"h": "Keep Walking in Him"},
 {"p": "Paul’s words are a perfect summary of this first chapter: **as you received Christ, so walk in Him.** You didn’t receive Him by being perfect, and you won’t walk with Him by being perfect either. You received Him by faith, and you’ll walk with Him the same way — one day at a time, one step at a time, one honest prayer at a time."},
 {"p": "Take a moment today to thank God for what He has done this week. Maybe a verse stood out to you. Maybe a prayer felt different. Maybe you noticed a little more peace than before. Don’t rush past it. **Gratitude waters the roots.**"},
 {"p": "**You are a tree planted by the water. Keep drinking from the source, and you will stay green in every season.**"},
 {"p": "Congratulations on completing Chapter 1! Tomorrow a new chapter begins: *Walking With Jesus* — where we’ll follow Him through the pages of the Gospels and discover what it means to walk closely with Him. Come back ready, and bring a thankful heart."}
],
}
